---
import ServicePage from '../../components/services/ServicePage.astro';
import { SERVICES } from '../../data/site';

export const prerender = true;

const canonical = 'https://busqueneil.com/services/ai-automation-services';
const svc = SERVICES[3];

const FAQ = [
  {
    q: 'What should we automate first?',
    a: "The task your team repeats most and complains about loudest. Usually that's lead intake into the CRM, follow-ups that depend on someone remembering, or a weekly report someone builds by hand. Show me that task, and I'll map it and price it.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Do we need AI, or a normal workflow?',
    a: "Often a normal workflow. If the steps never change, plain automation is cheaper and easier to trust. I add AI when the input is messy, a decision is needed or a person should review a draft.",
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens when a tool changes and a step breaks?',
    a: "Automations should alert you instead of failing quietly, so I build them that way. If you keep me on each month, I fix breaks as part of that work. If not, you get notes your team can follow.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Which tools do you build in?',
    a: "n8n, Zapier, Make and GoHighLevel for workflows, plus custom code when a job outgrows them. Everything runs in your own accounts.",
  },
];
---
<ServicePage
  title="AI Automation Services for Small Teams | Neil Busque"
  description="Neil Busque maps the repetitive work slowing your team down, then builds the AI agents and workflows that handle it. Based in New Jersey."
  canonical={canonical}
  heading="AI automation services for **small teams**."
  lead="I build automations for small teams in New Jersey, New York and across the US. The job is simple: find the repeated work that keeps stealing time, then build a dependable way to handle it."
  crumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', url: 'https://busqueneil.com/' },
    { name: 'Services', url: 'https://busqueneil.com/services' },
    { name: 'AI Automation Services', url: canonical },
  ]}
  topic="AI automation services"
  artSeed={svc.slug}
  artPalette="mist"
  summary={{ items: svc.items, facts: svc.facts }}
  related={['one-click-client-onboarding', 'meeting-notes-to-tasks']}
  relatedPalettes={['mist', 'navy']}
  faq={FAQ}
  service={{
    name: 'AI Automation Services',
    serviceType: ['AI automation', 'Workflow automation', 'Business process automation', 'CRM automation', 'AI agent development'],
    areaServed: ['New Jersey', 'New York', 'New York City', 'United States', 'Remote'],
  }}
>
  <h2>What I usually automate</h2>
  <p>Most teams don't need another big platform. They need a few specific jobs to stop depending on memory and copy and paste. These come up most often:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Leads.</strong> Capture, enrich, route and follow up automatically, so nothing sits in an inbox going cold.</li>
    <li><strong>Follow-up and nurture.</strong> Email and SMS sequences triggered by what people actually do, not by a calendar.</li>
    <li><strong>Meetings.</strong> Notes from a recorded call turned into tasks for your team, checked by a person first.</li>
    <li><strong>Ops and admin.</strong> Data entry, reporting, CRM upkeep and the handoffs between tools that quietly eat hours.</li>
    <li><strong>Research and content.</strong> Agents that read, summarize and draft on a schedule, so a person only has to edit.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Plain automation or an AI agent</h2>
  <p>Not everything needs AI. If the steps never change, a normal workflow is cheaper and easier to trust. I add an agent when the task involves messy input, a real decision or a draft a person should review. <a href="/services/ai-agent-development">More on AI agents</a>, and <a href="/guides/n8n-vs-zapier-vs-make">how I pick between n8n, Zapier and Make</a>.</p>

  <h2>How the work runs</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>We find the time sink.</strong> On a short call, we trace where the work slows down and what people keep doing by hand.</li>
    <li><strong>A clear scope and price.</strong> You see exactly what gets automated and what doesn't, before any work starts.</li>
    <li><strong>The first automation in days.</strong> You see it working on real data, and we adjust in short rounds.</li>
    <li><strong>You keep it, or I keep running it.</strong> Your choice. It's your system either way.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Why one person instead of an agency</h2>
  <p>Automations need looking after. A webhook changes, an API drops a field or your process changes. When that happens, you message the person who built it. There is no account manager in between.</p>

  <h2>Where I work</h2>
  <p>I'm based in New Jersey and work with teams across New Jersey and New York City, plus remote clients across the United States. Most of the work is remote wherever you are, and I'm reachable on US Eastern time.</p>

  <h2>See the work first</h2>
  <p>In 2026 I shipped more than 40 apps, AI agents and automations. Before that, I built more than 20 production n8n workflows across more than 10 client accounts at a marketing agency, including one-click client onboarding that cut a 30-day process to 7. Browse <a href="/work">the work</a> and <a href="/resources">the guides</a>, or see <a href="/services/ai-automation-consultant">what an AI automation consultant does</a>.</p>
</ServicePage>
